## Why This Is Asked

This question tests the ability to design extensible components using the Strategy and Registry patterns, along with understanding of thread-safety considerations and basic testing approaches for eviction correctness.

## Key Concepts

- **Strategy Pattern**: Encapsulate different eviction algorithms as interchangeable plug-ins
- **Runtime Registry**: Enable adding new policies without modifying existing code or restarting
- **Thread-Safety**: Considerations for concurrent access in JavaScript environments
- **Testing**: Basic approaches to verify eviction correctness

## Code Example

```typescript
interface EvictionPolicy<K> {
  onAccess(key: K, cache: Map<K, unknown>): void;
  onInsert(key: K, value: unknown, cache: Map<K, unknown>): void;
  evict(cache: Map<K, unknown>): K | null;
}

class PolicyRegistry<K> {
  private byName = new Map<string, EvictionPolicy<K>>();

  register(name: string, policy: EvictionPolicy<K>): void {
    this.byName.set(name, policy);
  }

  get(name: string): EvictionPolicy<K> | undefined {
    return this.byName.get(name);
  }
}

class Cache<K, V> {
  private store = new Map<K, V>();
  private registry: PolicyRegistry<K>;
  private policy: EvictionPolicy<K>;

  constructor(policy: EvictionPolicy<K>, registry?: PolicyRegistry<K>) {
    this.policy = policy;
    this.registry = registry || new PolicyRegistry();
  }

  set(key: K, value: V): void {
    if (this.store.size >= this.capacity) {
      const evicted = this.policy.evict(this.store);
      if (evicted) this.store.delete(evicted);
    }
    this.policy.onInsert(key, value, this.store);
    this.store.set(key, value);
  }

  get(key: K): V | undefined {
    const value = this.store.get(key);
    if (value !== undefined) {
      this.policy.onAccess(key, value, this.store);
    }
    return value;
  }

  registerPolicy(name: string, policy: EvictionPolicy<K>): void {
    this.registry.register(name, policy);
  }
}

// Usage
const lruPolicy: EvictionPolicy<string> = {
  onAccess: (key, cache) => { /* update access order */ },
  onInsert: (key, value, cache) => { /* track insertion */ },
  evict: (cache) => { /* return least recently used key */ }
};

const cache = new Cache(lruPolicy);
cache.set('a', 1);
cache.get('a');
```

## Trade-offs

| Policy | Pros | Cons |
|--------|------|------|
| **LRU** | Good hit rate for repeated access | Overhead tracking access order |
| **FIFO** | Simple, low overhead | Ignores access patterns |
| **TTL** | Automatic expiration, good for time-sensitive data | Requires background cleanup |

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the ability to design a small, extensible plugin system for logging using runtime registration and fault isolation.

## Key Concepts
- **Open-Closed Principle**: The Logger remains open for extension but closed for modification
- **Strategy/Plugin Patterns**: Each backend implements a common interface and can be swapped or added at runtime
- **Registry Pattern**: Enables runtime backend discovery and management by name
- **Asynchronous Dispatch**: Non-blocking logging ensures performance isn't impacted by slow backends
- **Fault Isolation**: A failing backend shouldn't crash the entire application

## Code Example
```typescript
interface Backend {
  name: string;
  log(level: LogLevel, msg: string, meta?: Record<string, unknown>): void | Promise<void>;
}

type LogLevel = 'debug' | 'info' | 'warn' | 'error';

class BackendRegistry {
  private backends = new Map<string, Backend>();

  register(name: string, backend: Backend): void {
    this.backends.set(name, backend);
  }

  get(name: string): Backend | undefined {
    return this.backends.get(name);
  }

  unregister(name: string): boolean {
    return this.backends.delete(name);
  }
}

class Logger {
  constructor(private registry: BackendRegistry) {}

  async log(level: LogLevel, msg: string, meta?: Record<string, unknown>): Promise<void> {
    const backends = Array.from(this.registry['backends'].values());
    await Promise.allSettled(
      backends.map(backend =>
        Promise.resolve(backend.log(level, msg, meta)).catch(() => {})
      )
    );
  }

  debug(msg: string, meta?: Record<string, unknown>): Promise<void> {
    return this.log('debug', msg, meta);
  }

  info(msg: string, meta?: Record<string, unknown>): Promise<void> {
    return this.log('info', msg, meta);
  }

  warn(msg: string, meta?: Record<string, unknown>): Promise<void> {
    return this.log('warn', msg, meta);
  }

  error(msg: string, meta?: Record<string, unknown>): Promise<void> {
    return this.log('error', msg, meta);
  }
}

// Usage Example
const registry = new BackendRegistry();

const consoleBackend: Backend = {
  name: 'console',
  log(level, msg, meta) {
    console.log(`[${level}] ${msg}`, meta || '');
  },
};

const fileBackend: Backend = {
  name: 'file',
  async log(level, msg, meta) {
    // Write to file implementation
  },
};

registry.register('console', consoleBackend);
registry.register('file', fileBackend);

const logger = new Logger(registry);
logger.info('Application started', { version: '1.0.0' });
```

